How can I make this design more mobile-friendly?
To make your design more mobile-friendly, you'll want to focus on responsiveness, flexible layouts, and touch-friendly elements. Here are some key improvements:

1. Use a Responsive Meta Tag
Make sure your HTML includes this viewport tag in the <head> section:

html
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
This ensures your website scales properly on different screen sizes.

2. Apply a Mobile-Friendly CSS Grid or Flexbox
Instead of fixed-width elements, use flexbox or CSS grid to make sections adjust dynamically.

css
main {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 20px;
}
For navigation, use a flexible layout:

css
nav ul {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: center;
}
3. Adjust Font Sizes and Padding for Mobile
Use relative units like em or rem instead of fixed pixels.

css
body {
    font-size: 1rem; /* Adjusts based on screen size */
    padding: 5vw;
}
4. Make Images and Tables Responsive
Ensure images resize properly:

css
img {
    max-width: 100%;
    height: auto;
}
Make your table scrollable on mobile:

css
table {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    overflow-x: auto;
}
5. Use Media Queries
Apply different styles for mobile users:

css
@media (max-width: 768px) {
    nav ul {
        flex-direction: column;
        align-items: center;
    }
    form input {
        width: 100%;
    }
}
6. Improve Touch Usability
Make buttons and links touch-friendly:

css
button, input[type="submit"] {
    padding: 12px 20px;
    font-size: 1.1em;
}
7. Optimize Load Speed
Use compressed images and minified CSS/JS to improve page speed.

