Can you explain more about media queries?
Absolutely! Media queries are a powerful feature of CSS that allow you to apply styles based on the size of the user's screen, device type, or other conditions. They're essential for creating responsive web designs that adapt seamlessly across different devices (mobile, tablet, desktop).

How Media Queries Work
Media queries use the @media rule to check for specific conditions and apply styles accordingly. Here’s a simple breakdown:

css
@media (max-width: 768px) {
    body {
        background-color: lightblue;
    }
}
This means: If the screen width is 768 pixels or smaller, change the background color to light blue.

If the screen width is larger than 768px, the normal styles will apply.

Common Media Query Breakpoints
These are standard screen sizes where media queries are commonly used:

css
/* Small devices (phones) */
@media (max-width: 600px) { ... }

/* Tablets */
@media (min-width: 601px) and (max-width: 1024px) { ... }

/* Laptops and small desktops */
@media (min-width: 1025px) and (max-width: 1440px) { ... }

/* Large screens */
@media (min-width: 1441px) { ... }
Example: Responsive Navigation Menu
Let’s say you have a navigation menu that should turn into a hamburger menu on mobile devices.

css
nav ul {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-around;
    list-style: none;
}

/* On mobile devices, stack the navigation links */
@media (max-width: 768px) {
    nav ul {
        flex-direction: column;
        align-items: center;
    }
}
Now, on screens smaller than 768px, the menu links will stack vertically instead of displaying in a horizontal row.

Other Uses for Media Queries
✔ Adjust font sizes for readability ✔ Hide/show elements depending on device type ✔ Change layout structure dynamically ✔ Improve usability for touch-based devices

