Can you provide real-world examples of media queries in use?
Absolutely! Media queries are widely used across the web to make designs adapt to different screen sizes and device types. Here are some real-world examples:

1. Responsive Navigation Menu
On desktop, navigation menus typically display horizontally. On mobile, they often collapse into a hamburger menu using media queries:

css
/* Default (Desktop) Navigation */
nav ul {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-around;
}

/* Mobile Navigation - Changes Layout */
@media (max-width: 768px) {
    nav ul {
        flex-direction: column;
        align-items: center;
    }
}
📱 When the screen is 768px or smaller, the menu stacks vertically.

2. Adjusting Font Sizes for Readability
Some websites increase font size on larger screens for better readability:

css
/* Default Font Size */
p {
    font-size: 16px;
}

/* Larger Screens (Desktop) */
@media (min-width: 1024px) {
    p {
        font-size: 20px;
    }
}
This ensures fonts aren’t too small on high-resolution monitors.

3. Hiding Elements on Mobile for Cleaner UI
Websites sometimes hide unnecessary elements on smaller screens to declutter the layout:

css
/* Default - Show Sidebar */
.sidebar {
    display: block;
}

/* Hide Sidebar on Mobile */
@media (max-width: 600px) {
    .sidebar {
        display: none;
    }
}
This improves the mobile experience by removing distractions.

4. Grid Layout Adjustments for Different Screens
Websites often change the number of columns displayed on different screen sizes:

css
.container {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
}

/* Reduce Columns on Smaller Screens */
@media (max-width: 768px) {
    .container {
        grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
}
🖥 Desktop: Displays 4 columns 📱 Mobile: Reduces to 2 columns for better spacing.

5. Image Scaling for Different Devices
High-resolution images can be resized dynamically to prevent unnecessary loading time:

css
img {
    max-width: 100%;
    height: auto;
}

/* Reduce Image Size on Mobile */
@media (max-width: 480px) {
    img {
        width: 80%;
    }
}
This ensures images don’t take up the entire screen on mobile devices.

Why Media Queries Matter?
✅ Better User Experience – Adjusts UI based on device ✅ Improved Performance – Reduces unnecessary elements on smaller screens ✅ Enhanced Readability – Ensures text is easy to read across different devices

